  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   We use a patch-based optimization method that iteratively improves the solution by adjusting small segments (patches) of the function. The key steps are:\n   - Use the interpolated parent solution as initial guess\n   - Clipping and constraint enforcement to enforce [0,1] bounds and integral\n   - Core optimization phase (greedy search and regular search) \n   - Patch-based improvement to escape local optima\n   The algorithm balances cost and effectiveness by using smaller patches and linear interpolation. The optimizer uses Nelder-Mead for greedy updates followed by SLSQP for constrained optimization. We validate the solution by checking boundaries and integral. The method improves by exploring local variations with guaranteed constraint satisfaction.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   Representation: Piecewise constant function on [0,2] with n_points samples\n   Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^{n_points}\n   Objective: Minimize C5 = max_k \u222b h(x) (1-h(x+k)) dx\n   Implemented using: \n     corr = np.correlate(h_clip, 1-h_clip, mode=\"full\")\n     C5 = np.max(corr) * (2/n_points)\n   Constraints:\n     Integral: sum(h) = n_points/2\n     Boundaries: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n   Repair procedure:\n     Clip values to [0,1] and then scale the entire vector to enforce integral\n   Search strategy:\n     Three variant of the greedy search with adaptive patch selection sizes\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Validate parent solution if available\n   - Precompute butterfly interpolation pattern for patch generation\n   - Define objective function as before\n   - Core optimization phase (greedy search)\n        `- Want: balance exploration (large patches) and exploitation (small patches)\n        `- For each candidate solution:\n             * Select random contiguous segment based on patch size strategy\n             * Clip values to satisfy [0,1] bounds\n             * Compute objective\n             * Repair constraints by scaling\n             * Update solution if improved\n   - Check time budget frequently\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n   - Patch size strategy: \n        - Primary: adaptive from 8->12 samples points\n        - Attempts from 8->12 samples points\n        - Softness parameter: 0.25\n        - Noise std: 0.01\n        - Probability for large patches: 0.3\n        - Noise std: 0.01\n        - Bounds check after repair: yes\n   - Repair method: clip then scale entire vector\n   - Initial strategy: 20 greedy search iterations\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - Dominant cost: O(n_points^2) correlation computations\n   - Estimated time: ~45s for 280 points (including repair)\n   - Feasibility restoration: parent fallback if optimization fails\n   - Budget monitoring: at each objective evaluation\n   - Clean fallback: if repair fails, scale the parent solution\n   - SLSQP for first optimization, then Nelder-Mead\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Check h[i] in [0,1] for all i\n   - Ensure np.sum(h) = n_points/2\n   - Objective must be finite\n   - Return (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n   - Evaluation of parent's best score separately\n   - Base case: if time expired, return best candidate",
